&lt;p&gt;Picture two shoppers. One asks ChatGPT, “Are Nikes good shoes for marathon runners?” The other asks, “What’s the best running shoe?” Both want top-notch athletic shoes. Only one specifically wants to know about Nikes. That’s the idea behind branded vs. unbranded queries in AI search.&lt;/p&gt;  
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=9dd5e54b-fbef-4dd0-bc44-1689feb1ea18&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Get Started with HubSpot's AEO Tool" height="58" width="335" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/9dd5e54b-fbef-4dd0-bc44-1689feb1ea18.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;But why does this small difference matter for your brand? A branded query comes from someone who already knows you. They’re checking you out, comparing you with a rival, or even confirming a choice. AI’s answer can shape how someone sees you right before they decide.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;An unbranded query comes from someone who’s still shopping. They haven’t picked a brand yet, so if the AI names you, you’re in the running. But if it doesn’t, you may never get a shot. In AI search, unbranded queries are where new buyers meet you, or don’t if you don’t play your cards right.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Each type reaches a different audience and tells you something different about your performance. Optimize for and track only one, and you reach half the audience and get half the story.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Don’t believe me? In November 2025, Google added a &lt;a href="https://developers.google.com/search/blog/2025/11/search-console-branded-filter"&gt;branded queries filter to Search Console&lt;/a&gt; that automatically separates branded and unbranded &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/how-to-do-keyword-research-ht"&gt;search queries and keywords&lt;/a&gt;. AI search needs the same split. It also needs a plan to track both.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;In this guide, you’ll learn how to:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Tell branded and unbranded prompts apart.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Run a simple tracking routine each week.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Read your results without fooling yourself.&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Let’s start with the basics.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Table of Contents&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#what-are-branded-vs-unbranded-queries-in-ai-search"&gt;What are branded vs. unbranded queries in AI search?&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#why-branded-vs-unbranded-queries-change-how-you-measure-aeo"&gt;Why Branded vs. Unbranded Queries Change How You Measure AEO&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#how-to-run-prompt-tracking-measure-for-branded-vs-unbranded-queries"&gt;How to Run Prompt Tracking &amp;amp; Measure for Branded vs. Unbranded Queries&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#how-to-interpret-branded-vs-unbranded-results-and-share-of-visibility"&gt;How to Interpret Branded vs. Unbranded Results and Share of Visibility&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#how-hubspot-aeo-scales-prompt-tracking-for-branded-vs-unbranded-queries"&gt;How HubSpot AEO Scales Prompt Tracking for Branded vs. Unbranded Queries&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#frequently-asked-questions-about-branded-vs-unbranded-queries"&gt;Frequently Asked Questions About Branded vs. Unbranded Queries&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="#keep-track-of-both-shoppers-and-queries"&gt;Keep track of both shoppers and queries.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;What are branded vs. unbranded queries in AI search?&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;branded query&lt;/strong&gt; names a brand. That can be your company name, a close variation, a common misspelling, or one of your product names. For example, “Are Nikes good shoes for marathon runners?” is a branded query.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;So is “Are Air Force 1s good running shoes?” as they include a Nike product. Branded queries are valuable because they indicate that users are aware of your brand and are &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/ai-for-branding"&gt;using AI to research it&lt;/a&gt; specifically.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;An &lt;strong&gt;unbranded query&lt;/strong&gt;, on the other hand, names no brand. It describes a category, a problem, or a use case. “What’s the best shoe for marathon runners?” is unbranded. Unbranded queries are valuable in their own right, as appearing in them can expose you to a new audience of potential customers.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These types of queries aren’t new to AI or answer engines. &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/blog/tabid/6307/bid/5645/what-do-coke-pepsi-pampers-dominos-google-and-facebook-have-in-common.aspx"&gt;Branded search&lt;/a&gt; has always meant people looking for you by name. Google even built the idea into &lt;a href="https://developers.google.com/search/blog/2025/11/search-console-branded-filter"&gt;Search Console&lt;/a&gt;, where a filter now sorts the two for you.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://blog.hubspot.com/hubfs/blogbot/branded-vs-unbranded-queries-1-20261103-7981637.webp" style="margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto; display: block; width: 650px; height: auto; max-width: 100%;" alt="Google Search Console filter dialog showing branded vs unbranded queries options"&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p style="text-align: center; font-size: 12px;"&gt;&lt;a href="https://developers.google.com/search/blog/2025/11/search-console-branded-filter"&gt;&lt;em&gt;Source&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Queries vs. prompts: What’s the difference?&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;You’ll notice two words throughout this guide: queries and prompts. They sound alike, but they’re not quite the same.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;In traditional search, people type short &lt;strong&gt;queries&lt;/strong&gt;, like “best running shoes” or “Nike shoes.” In AI search, they write longer, chattier &lt;strong&gt;prompts&lt;/strong&gt;, like “Which brand makes the best shoes for running a marathon?”&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Here’s how we’ll use them. “Query” is the general idea. “Prompt” is the exact question you track in an answer engine like ChatGPT, Gemini, or Perplexity.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;There are also three types of unbranded prompts.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;h3&gt;Three Kinds of Unbranded Prompts&lt;/h3&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;A branded prompt is easy to spot. It names a brand. Unbranded prompts take a little more care, because they come in three flavors. Each one matches a different moment in the buying process. The table below puts them next to a branded prompt so you can see the contrast.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pro tip:&lt;/strong&gt; Watch out for prompts that name only a competitor, like “Mailchimp alternatives.” They’re not unbranded, because they name a brand. They’re not yours either. Give them their own label so they don’t skew your numbers later.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;If you can sort a prompt into one of these four rows in five seconds, you’re ready for the rest of this guide.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Note: One more bit of vocabulary. We use “AI search” and answer engine optimization (AEO) to mean the same thing as you adapt to this &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/adapting-to-the-new-era-of-search"&gt;new era of search&lt;/a&gt;. And the goal with both is to show up inside AI-written answers.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;So, why does this sorting matter so much? Because branded and unbranded prompts behave very differently in AI answers. Mix them, and your results get misleading. Let’s look at why.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;Why Branded vs. Unbranded Queries Change How You Measure AEO&lt;/...

&lt;p&gt;Most obstacles in marketing automation aren’t actually automation problems. 86.4% of marketing teams now use AI somewhere in their workflows. Yet, measuring ROI still tops the challenge list at 33%, according to HubSpot’s 2026 &lt;a href="https://www.hubspot.com/state-of-marketing"&gt;State of Marketing Report&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;  
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=475547db-03b5-4722-b941-be5dca650abf&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Get Started with HubSpot's Agent Builder" height="58" width="373" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/475547db-03b5-4722-b941-be5dca650abf.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Ultimately, marketing automation issues are rarely tool problems. They are most often caused by &lt;strong&gt;unclear strategy, bad data, siloed tools, and weak team alignment. &lt;/strong&gt;The automation platform executes those problems faster and at greater scale.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;This guide diagnoses the seven most common marketing automation challenges, what breaks, and the specific fixes. Use it to identify and repair underperforming automation workflows.&lt;/p&gt; 
