&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;Why Obstacles in Marketing Automation Start With Strategy&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Most obstacles in marketing automation trace back to a missing or unclear go-to-market strategy. Teams that automate without defined goals and lifecycle journey logic end up with more signals, like emails, branches, and alerts. But, that doesn’t result in more sales pipeline. The symptoms are easy to spot once someone names them:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Every contact receives the same nurture track regardless of lifecycle stage or ICP fit.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Workflows accumulate for years because nobody documented why they exist or whether they should stay on.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Campaign volume rises while conversion rates stay flat.&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;These obstacles in marketing automation are &lt;strong&gt;strategy failures. &lt;/strong&gt;They sit behind many of the classic &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/marketing-automation-mistakes"&gt;marketing automation mistakes&lt;/a&gt; teams repeat every year.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Part of the confusion is that automation itself has changed. &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/workflow-automation"&gt;Modern workflow automation&lt;/a&gt; is no longer a backend ops function. Instead, AI-augmented workflows have turned it into a frontline growth lever that reads context (buyer behavior, persona, funnel stage) and adjusts the next best action. A tool with that much reach deserves a real strategy behind it.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;So the fix? &lt;strong&gt;Always start with strategy&lt;/strong&gt;. Before building any automation, the team should first define the following.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Why (Goals):&lt;/strong&gt; Every automation exists to move a business metric, whether that’s a revenue goal, an efficiency goal, or an engagement goal. This is also where the team should debate what to automate versus what deliberately not to automate.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Who (Clear, Aligned Segmentation):&lt;/strong&gt; A highly personalized experience is why 93.2% of marketers say personalized, segmented experiences drive more leads and purchases (2026 State of Marketing).&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The How (Entry, Exit, and Suppression Criteria):&lt;/strong&gt; Who enters, what removes them, and which audiences must never be enrolled? Most “automation gone rogue” incidents are missing exit criteria.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;The Owner: &lt;/strong&gt;A named human should be accountable for each automation’s performance and ongoing maintenance. As the strategy evolves, so should automation.&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;In 2026, marketing automation strategy also means deciding what AI is allowed to do versus where a human stays in the loop. Traditional rule-based workflows execute predefined steps. Now, AI agents can independently research, draft, and decide what to do. Teams need an explicit policy for which decisions stay human &lt;em&gt;before&lt;/em&gt; they scale automation.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;a&gt;&lt;/a&gt; 
&lt;h2&gt;Obstacles in marketing automation come from bad data.&lt;/h2&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Remember: an automation platform is only as good as the CRM records underneath it. Duplicate contacts could trigger duplicate sequences. Stale job titles tend to route leads to the wrong owner. Missing lifecycle properties enroll customers in prospect nurtures. Every one of these is a high-stakes mistake no marketer wants attached to their funnel. Bad data causes poor segmentation, broken personalization, and incorrect lead routing.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The scope of the problem is bigger than most teams admit: Only 65% of marketers say they have high-quality data about their target audience, per HubSpot’s 2026 State of Marketing report. This number hasn’t improved year over year even as AI adoption soared.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;Part of the issue is that “clean” is no longer enough in today’s marketing landscape. The database also has to be &lt;em&gt;complete&lt;/em&gt; in the dimensions modern AI-ready automation actually uses. A &lt;strong&gt;revenue-ready B2B database&lt;/strong&gt; spans five signal types:&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;ul&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Demographic data, like contacts and job titles.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Firmographic data, like company size, industry, and geography.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Technographic data, or the tools and account runs.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;Event-triggered data, like executive hires, funding, and acquisitions.&lt;/li&gt; 
 &lt;li&gt;And intent data.&lt;/li&gt; 
&lt;/ul&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;The trap most marketing teams fall into in today’s marketing is &lt;a href="https://www.nextgenabm.io/post/weekly-abm-abx-insights-is-your-marketing-database-abm-ready%23:~:text%3Dstruggle%2520to%2520consolidate,a%2520top%2520priority."&gt;collection without consolidation&lt;/a&gt;. Sales teams still struggle to interpret the signals because they arrive fragmented across tools instead of...

&lt;p&gt;If you’re weighing Scrunch vs Ahrefs, you’re probably asking a bigger question underneath it: do I buy a specialist tool for AI search, or let the SEO platform I already use handle it?&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;People are asking AI tools for recommendations before they ever hit a website, and when Google shows an AI summary, searchers &lt;a href="https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2025/07/22/google-users-are-less-likely-to-click-on-links-when-an-ai-summary-appears-in-the-results/"&gt;end their browsing session more often&lt;/a&gt;, according to Pew Research Center. So it makes sense that you want to see how your brand shows up in those answers.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;a class="cta_button" href="https://www.hubspot.com/cs/ci/?pg=9dd5e54b-fbef-4dd0-bc44-1689feb1ea18&amp;amp;pid=53&amp;amp;ecid=&amp;amp;hseid=&amp;amp;hsic="&gt;&lt;img class="hs-cta-img " style="height: auto !important; width: auto !important; max-width: 100% !important;border-width: 0px; /*hs-extra-styles*/; margin: 0 auto; display: block; margin-top: 20px; margin-bottom: 20px" alt="Get Started with HubSpot's AEO Tool" height="58" width="335" src="https://no-cache.hubspot.com/cta/default/53/9dd5e54b-fbef-4dd0-bc44-1689feb1ea18.png" align="middle"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;Here’s the quick version. Scrunch is built for AEO. It tracks your AI visibility and also changes what AI bots see when they crawl your site. Ahrefs Brand Radar adds AI brand monitoring and citation tracking to the keyword, backlink, and site audit tools you may already use. If you’re starting a dedicated AEO program, Scrunch is worth a close look. If your team lives in Ahrefs, Brand Radar will feel like home. Either way, AEO works alongside &lt;a href="https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/ai-seo"&gt;AI SEO&lt;/a&gt; and traditional SEO, so you don’t have to pick one world over the other.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;We’ll compare the two on engine and surface coverage, prompt methodology, citation and reporting detail, actionability, integrations, pricing, and best-fit team.&lt;/p&gt; 
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;One more thing: HubSpot sells its own AEO products, and will&amp;nbsp;show up after the neutral comparison, judged on the same criteria.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt; 
